{
  "subtype": "causal_attribution",
  "outcome_variable": "visit_duration_min",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "primary_complaint_type",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "The nature of the presenting complaint (chronic vs acute/emergency) plausibly drives both what treatment/workup occurs and how long the visit takes; a pre-encounter characteristic, so a genuine common cause rather than a mediator."
    },
    {
      "name": "diagnostic_workup_required",
      "causal_role": "mediator",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Ordering extensive labs/imaging is on the causal path to longer duration and is itself a consequence of the clinical presentation; adjusting for it would block part of the effect being attributed. Mediator, not confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "multisystem_involvement",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Underlying multisystem disease is a patient/clinical characteristic that raises the need for workup/intervention AND lengthens the visit; a plausible common cause surfaced from data."
    },
    {
      "name": "vital_sign_abnormality_present",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Abnormal vitals reflect patient severity, plausibly driving both intervention decisions and visit length; a pre-treatment severity indicator acting as a common cause."
    },
    {
      "name": "procedure_or_intervention_performed",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "This is a candidate cause whose effect on duration is being attributed -- performing an in-office/invasive procedure directly consumes visit time. Serves as the treatment."
    },
    {
      "name": "medication_initiation_or_adjustment",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Another candidate clinical action whose effect on duration is under attribution; starting/adjusting medication adds counseling and documentation time."
    },
    {
      "name": "patient_age_group",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Age influences disease complexity and the treatments pursued as well as visit pacing/duration; a classic common cause. Note it duplicates the existing age_group column, but semantically a confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "visit_type",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Whether a visit is a new problem, follow-up, or preventive shapes both what interventions occur and how long the encounter runs; a structural pre-encounter common cause."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["procedure_or_intervention_performed", "medication_initiation_or_adjustment"],
  "confounders": [
    {"name": "primary_complaint_type", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.8},
    {"name": "multisystem_involvement", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.75},
    {"name": "vital_sign_abnormality_present", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.7},
    {"name": "patient_age_group", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.6},
    {"name": "visit_type", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.7}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.65,
  "rationale": "The added columns do set up a causal estimation: they supply candidate treatments (procedure/intervention performed, medication initiation/adjustment) whose effect on visit_duration is being attributed, and several genuine confounders that are pre-treatment common causes of both the clinical actions taken and the visit length -- primary_complaint_type, multisystem_involvement, vital_sign_abnormality_present, and visit_type. None of these were named in the query (which only says 'clinical factors'), so they demonstrate real surfacing of confounders from the domain. Weaknesses: diagnostic_workup_required is a mediator on the treatment->outcome path and should not be adjusted for; patient_age_group merely duplicates the existing age_group; and the operator does not cleanly separate a single treatment from confounders, mixing multiple candidate causes. Overall a partially successful realisation of causal attribution with meaningful non-query confounders but a mediator contamination that lowers quality."
}
